A||||7||jch82||Center Console Repair???||||5||
Z||000000||jch82||04-05-2001||10:47 PM||||I have almost my entire interior tore out. Couldn't believe how easy it was! It was an utter disaster in there though. There was so much crap under the seats I could barely see the carpet. A plus to this was I must have found at least $10 in change!! Anyways, a couple of the center console pieces are flaring at the edges (hope you guys know what I'm talking about) and I was wondering what type of glue I should use to fix this slight dilemma....Are they even worth fixing or shouold I try to find different ones in better shape?? Thanks<P>If anyone is interested in the console piece with the glovebox in it (covering the ECM), let me know. The box works great and is in really nice condition but there is about a 4" rip in the bottom half of the console itself (door is in great condition). Would also consider taking the glovebox out if someone wanted just that. Email me and I can get you a pic of it. BTW, it is grey.<P>Josh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Yeah, my girlfriend takes collect calls from the road<BR>And it doesn't seem to matter that I'm lacking in the bulge<BR>She laughs at my dumb jokes when no one does<BR>She brings me mexican food from Sombrero's just because<BR>                 <I>-Blink 182</I>||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroJoe||04-05-2001||11:19 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>I have almost my entire interior tore out.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds just like me!<P>I used acetone to clean all the foam crap off, and then used super glue and super glue accelerator to glue it back together.  I tried many types of glue, but they all eventually peeled back and were just a waste of time.<P>If you use the accelerator you wont have to use clamps, just hold it by hand (careful not to get any on ya) until it dries, about 10 seconds.  Do a little section at a time.  I did this on all of my vinyl pieces, they came out really great!<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||v8fieroGT||04-05-2001||11:21 PM||||Hey, I just did the same thing to my car.All i did was clean the edges where i was going to glue with rubbing alcohol.Let it dry for about an hour cause the foam inside gets wet.Then all i used was super glue.I needed 6 tubes for a decent size job. I used a lot of clamps and most of the edges you will have to keep in place with your fingers for about a minute to let the glue settle cause the clamps wont hold it in the right shape.It takes a while but it came out perfect.I was suprised how good it came out.Make sure that you dry clamp first to see how and in what shape the clamps will hold it.But your fingers are the best clamp.Good luck with it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ArchMagi||04-05-2001||11:55 PM||||I have that problem with my 87.  The console piece (were my radio is) is cracked (on the back) about a good 8 inchs along the drivers side.  <P>Looks like im getting some new super glue!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||04-06-2001||12:01 AM||||you can get lot bigger tubes of super glue at hobby shops. they make diff ones that dry faster or slower. Also different thicknesses, from thin to thick gel. They also have different tips, some are long to get in hard to reach places.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfixit58||04-06-2001||07:33 AM||||Go to your nearest body shop supply store (i.e., paint store) and by the 3M glue used to replace body molding. It's like a super concentrated Super Glue. It bonds quickly and is not effected by heat.<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000006||Taijiguy||04-06-2001||08:57 AM||||3M Super Weatherstrip Adhesive. Never seen anything that holds like it. Most tenacious stuff ever made I think.<p>[This message has been edited by Taijiguy (edited 04-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Frank Sombers||04-06-2001||09:51 AM||||JCH82<BR>Before applying glue try clamping the console <BR>together for a couple of days (just like you are going to glue, but WITHOUT the glue). This will allow the vinyl padding to develop a "memory" regarding its original shape. When you do this you won't have to fight to get and keep the proper position for gluing as much. <BR> ||||reg||3
